---
/**
 * THE INDEX — slice R8. The funnel spread, five destinations.
 *
 * CONTEXT.md Section Composition table, row "Index 01-05":
 *
 *   Entrance      Numerals already at display scale
 *   Column window 1-11
 *   Image         none
 *   Rhythm        standard
 *   Margin holds  — (empty)
 *   Length        index only
 *
 * THE ENTRANCE IS THE NUMERALS AND NOTHING IS ADDED ON TOP OF THEM.
 * No kicker, no hairline head, no visible section title, no decorative
 * mark, no photograph, no live-margin content. The table says the
 * entrance IS the numerals at display scale; an entrance device laid
 * over an entrance is two entrances.
 *
 * ---- THE LEDE (R8.1) --------------------------------------------
 * CONTEXT.md's lede rule is unconditional — "every content section
 * pulls one sentence out and sets it in Fraunces at display scale. No
 * exceptions." R8 shipped the Index as the one content section on the
 * page without one, and a reader arriving off the pivot got five names
 * and no statement of what the list is.
 *
 * IT IS NOT A KICKER AND MUST NOT BECOME ONE. Once R12 mounts the
 * folio, "INDEX" prints down the left viewport edge for this section's
 * whole height; a Montserrat INDEX kicker at column 1 would set the
 * folio's own label a second time, two inches away, and would put a
 * second entrance device above the numerals that ARE the entrance.
 *
 * IT SITS ON .idx-body — the same window, the same left edge, as the
 * five names — so it heads the column it actually heads rather than
 * introducing a fourth left edge. It takes the page's shared
 * --lede-size, so it is the same size as every other lede on the page.
 *
 * THE STRING IS NEW COPY AND IS NOT IN COPY.md. It ships pending
 * Jackson's sign-off, on the same footing as the "THREE SONS, THREE
 * PROGRAMS" caption R6.3 flagged. COPY.md was NOT edited to add it.
 *
 * IT IS THE FIRST SECTION AFTER THE PIVOT. Everything above the
 * Feature Quote is her own work; everything below it is this. The
 * Index is the page's navigation, and it is the only section flush to
 * the sheet's left trim across its full width — see the window note in
 * the stylesheet for why that is what makes it read as a contents page
 * rather than as another section.
 *
 * ---- COPY -------------------------------------------------------
 * Verbatim from COPY.md, "Index". Five rows, in COPY.md's order:
 *
 *   01  Premier Leadership            Consulting & leadership development
 *   02  Everyday Legends Foundation   Education, athletics & service
 *   03  Educational Leadership        Alternative education high school principal
 *   04  The McClain Brothers          Jaylen, KJ & Cam
 *   05  Charging It to the Game       The blog
 *
 * THIS NUMBERING IS THE INDEX'S OWN. It is unrelated to page order, to
 * src/data/sections.ts's folio numerals, and to the decorative marks in
 * the abridged sections. CONTEXT.md's "Decorative marks vs. Index
 * numbers" rule: the three are unrelated typographic devices and MUST
 * NOT be made to agree. This has been flagged four times.
 *
 * ENTRY 01 READS "PREMIER LEADERSHIP", NOT "PREMIER LEADERSHIP, LLC".
 * The old build's FunnelIndex.astro carries the suffix; it does not
 * travel. COPY.md's resolved naming decision puts the legal form in
 * exactly one place on the site — the About paragraph, where the
 * client wrote it herself.
 *
 * THE UPPERCASE ON THE DESCRIPTIONS IS A text-transform, a visual
 * treatment. The authored strings are set in sentence case exactly as
 * COPY.md writes them, so a copy diff against the deck is a string
 * comparison and not a case-folding exercise.
 *
 * ---- DESTINATIONS -----------------------------------------------
 * 01/02/03 are outbound links, new tab, rel="noopener". NOT
 * "noopener noreferrer": anchor-digital-standards.md is the
 * authoritative cross-repo standard and noreferrer strips the Referer
 * header, costing Anchor referral attribution from every client site
 * it ships.
 *
 * 04 IS NOT A LINK. It is the <summary> of the expansion below it.
 *
 * 05 IS AN IN-PAGE ANCHOR, same tab, no rel. Its href is taken from
 * src/data/sections.ts rather than invented — the blog section's id in
 * that list is `charging-it-to-the-game`, so the anchor is
 * `#charging-it-to-the-game`. The section itself is R9 and does not
 * exist yet, so the anchor resolves to nothing today; that is expected
 * and is why the id is READ from the list rather than guessed. R9
 * mounts a <section> carrying that exact id.
 *
 * ---- THE EXPANSION IS NATIVE <details> --------------------------
 * NO JAVASCRIPT. No scroll listener, no observer, no toggle script, no
 * ARIA state management. <details>/<summary> handles aria-expanded
 * itself, and CONTEXT.md's "one scroll listener exists in the entire
 * build" budget is spent on the folio (R12), not on a disclosure.
 *
 * CLOSED ON LOAD. CONTEXT.md's "all content visible on load" rule is
 * about scroll-reveals and opacity-0 initial states — content that a
 * full-page capture or a client-generated PDF would render blank. The
 * three brothers are content behind a control the reader operates, not
 * content hidden from them, and the control announces itself. The old
 * build shipped `open` for the capture reason; that reasoning does not
 * survive the rule being read for what it is.
 *
 * ---- THE THREE BROTHERS -----------------------------------------
 * Data only, no prose. COPY.md gives three columns — name, program,
 * website — and three columns ship. No Instagram handle, no X handle,
 * no jersey graphic, no headshot, no class year beyond the one COPY.md
 * puts inside Cam's program string.
 *
 * TWO DOMAINS LINK, ONE DOES NOT, AND THAT IS A MEASUREMENT (R8.1).
 * CONTEXT.md's standing rule conditions the click on LIVENESS, not on
 * preference: "if an individual site is not live, render the domain as
 * plain text, not as a link that goes nowhere." R8 shipped all three as
 * plain text because liveness was unconfirmed. It was confirmed in R8.1
 * by request, per domain:
 *
 *   jaylenmcclain.com  200, real site — nav reads About Jaylen / Family
 *                      and Football / Jaylen in the Media / Jaylen's
 *                      Locker Room; meta description "The website of
 *                      Jaylen McClain".            -> LIVE, links
 *   kjmcclain.com      200, real site — "KJ McClain", "2026 Early
 *                      Enrollee - the University of Tennessee", "State
 *                      Champ to the SEC", a full bio paragraph.
 *                                                  -> LIVE, links
 *   cammcclain.com     200, but the served page is a HOLDING PAGE:
 *                      "Launching Soon" over an email-capture form. The
 *                      string "Cam McClain" does not appear anywhere in
 *                      the HTML.                   -> NOT LIVE, stays
 *                                                     plain text
 *
 * A 200 IS NOT THE TEST. All three return 200 from the same GoDaddy
 * host; two serve the athlete's site and one serves a coming-soon page,
 * and a click that lands a reader on "Launching Soon" is exactly the
 * dead click the rule exists to prevent while printed material is
 * driving traffic. Cam's domain is NOT wrapped in <a>.
 *
 * VISUAL TREATMENT IS IDENTICAL EITHER WAY — same face, size, tracking,
 * case, colour and alignment on all three. Only the click differs. When
 * cammcclain.com ships real content, the fix is to wrap that one string
 * and nothing else changes.
 *
 * THE DOMAIN STRING IS THE CLICK TARGET, not the brother's row and not
 * the name. The expansion is inside a <summary>'s sibling, so a row-wide
 * link would nest interactive content inside a disclosure and give a
 * keyboard user two targets for one visual line.
 *
 * OSU INTERIM RULE, ALL THREE SCHOOLS. School names as plain text
 * only. No logos, no marks, no team photography, no school-keyed
 * colour. Jaylen's 2026 Ohio State captaincy is NOT surfaced — the
 * client has made no public announcement.
 *
 * ---- NO GOLD ----------------------------------------------------
 * CONTEXT.md's gold budget, read as R7 left it: saturated type accents
 * are 3 of 3 and genuinely spent. The hairline-gold bucket reads 0 of
 * 8 — but 8 is a CEILING rather than a spend, and five rules in one
 * navigational section would consume most of that ceiling for the
 * page's least emphatic content. The Index's rules are Smoked Slate.
 * The old build's index drew every rule in gold; that does not travel
 * either.
 */
import { sections } from '../data/sections.ts';

/* The blog anchor is READ from the section list, never typed. If R9
   ships the blog under a different id, this breaks loudly at build
   time rather than shipping a silently dead anchor. */
const blog = sections.find((s) => s.label === 'Charging It to the Game');
if (!blog) {
  throw new Error(
    'R8: no "Charging It to the Game" entry in src/data/sections.ts — ' +
      'the Index cannot derive entry 05’s anchor.'
  );
}
const blogHref = `#${blog.id}`;
---

<section class="sheet index" id="sec-index" aria-labelledby="index-heading">
  {/* The section announces itself through the numerals and the rules,
      not through a title — so the <h2> is visually hidden via the .vh
      primitive (position:absolute + clip-path:inset(50%)), never
      display:none, which would take it out of the accessibility tree.
      The string matches this section's label in src/data/sections.ts
      exactly, per CONTEXT.md's locked heading structure: each
      section's <h2> is the same string the folio uses. */}
  <h2 class="vh" id="index-heading">Index</h2>

  {/* An <ol>, because the entries are an ordered list and the numerals
      are its numbering. The displayed numerals are authored text
      carrying aria-hidden="true": the list is ALREADY ordered, and
      "oh one" read before every name is noise. */}
  {/* THE LEDE. On .idx-body, the names' own window and left edge — the
      primitive is reused, nothing hand-sets grid-column. Authored
      before the <ol>, so grid's sparse cursor seats it in its own row:
      it takes columns 4-11 of row 1, leaving the cursor past column 11,
      and the <ol> asks for column 1, which is behind the cursor and
      therefore drops to row 2. No row number is declared anywhere. */}
  <p class="idx-body idx-lede">Where the work continues.</p>

  {/* An <ol>, because the entries are an ordered list and the numerals
      are its numbering. The displayed numerals are authored text
      carrying aria-hidden="true": the list is ALREADY ordered, and
      "oh one" read before every name is noise.

      THE LIST'S TOP EDGE IS THE <ol>'s OWN border-top (R8.1), not a
      separate element. The <ol> already spans the section window
      exactly, so the rule needs no new primitive and no new markup, and
      the clearance BELOW it is entry 01's row padding-top — structurally
      the same quantity that sits below every other rule in the section,
      rather than a second value kept in agreement by hand. */}
  <ol class="idx-window idx-list">
    <li class="idx-item">
      <a
        class="idx-row"
        href="https://www.premierleadersllc.com"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener"
      >
        <span class="idx-num" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
        <span class="idx-body">
          <span class="idx-line"><span class="idx-name">Premier Leadership</span></span>
          <span class="idx-desc">Consulting &amp; leadership development</span>
        </span>
      </a>
    </li>

    <li class="idx-item">
      <a
        class="idx-row"
        href="https://www.everydaylegend.com"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener"
      >
        <span class="idx-num" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
        <span class="idx-body">
          <span class="idx-line"><span class="idx-name">Everyday Legends Foundation</span></span>
          <span class="idx-desc">Education, athletics &amp; service</span>
        </span>
      </a>
    </li>

    <li class="idx-item">
      <a class="idx-row" href="https://www.ucvts.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        <span class="idx-num" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
        <span class="idx-body">
          <span class="idx-line"><span class="idx-name">Educational Leadership</span></span>
          <span class="idx-desc">Alternative education high school principal</span>
        </span>
      </a>
    </li>

    {/* 04 — THE EXPANSION. The whole row is the <summary>; entry 04 is
        not a link. The rule that closes this entry sits on the <li>,
        BELOW the <details>, so it moves down when the expansion opens.
        That is correct: the rule closes the entry, not the summary. */}
    <li class="idx-item">
      <details class="idx-details">
        <summary class="idx-row">
          <span class="idx-num" aria-hidden="true">04</span>
          <span class="idx-body">
            <span class="idx-line">
              <span class="idx-name">The McClain Brothers</span>
              {/* The indicator. Entry 04 is the only row carrying one —
                  it is the only row whose behaviour differs, and the
                  four link rows need no glyph to announce that they are
                  links. The glyph itself is generated content so the
                  +/- swap is a CSS state and not a script; the span is
                  aria-hidden because <details> already exposes
                  aria-expanded. NO rotation, NO transition, NO
                  transform. */}
              <span class="idx-sign" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            </span>
            <span class="idx-desc">Jaylen, KJ &amp; Cam</span>
          </span>
        </summary>

        {/* Placed on .idx-body, so the brothers start on the same left
            edge as the names above them. NO sub-columns and NO new left
            edges — CONTEXT.md's three-edges rule holds inside this
            section as strictly as it holds outside it. */}
        <div class="idx-body idx-expand">
          <ul class="idx-bros">
            <li class="idx-bro">
              <span class="idx-bro-line">
                <span class="idx-bro-name">Jaylen McClain</span>
                {/* LIVE — verified R8.1. */}
                <a
                  class="idx-bro-site idx-bro-link"
                  href="https://jaylenmcclain.com"
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="noopener">jaylenmcclain.com</a>
              </span>
              <span class="idx-bro-prog">Ohio State Football | #8</span>
            </li>
            <li class="idx-bro">
              <span class="idx-bro-line">
                <span class="idx-bro-name">KJ McClain</span>
                {/* LIVE — verified R8.1. */}
                <a
                  class="idx-bro-site idx-bro-link"
                  href="https://kjmcclain.com"
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="noopener">kjmcclain.com</a>
              </span>
              <span class="idx-bro-prog">University of Tennessee Football | #40</span>
            </li>
            <li class="idx-bro">
              <span class="idx-bro-line">
                <span class="idx-bro-name">Cam McClain</span>
                {/* NOT LIVE — serves a "Launching Soon" holding page as
                    of R8.1. Plain text, NOT an <a>. Wrap this one string
                    when the site ships; nothing else changes. */}
                <span class="idx-bro-site">cammcclain.com</span>
              </span>
              <span class="idx-bro-prog">Seton Hall Prep Football | #6 · Class of 2028</span>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>
    </li>

    <li class="idx-item">
      <a class="idx-row" href={blogHref}>
        <span class="idx-num" aria-hidden="true">05</span>
        <span class="idx-body">
          <span class="idx-line"><span class="idx-name">Charging It to the Game</span></span>
          <span class="idx-desc">The blog</span>
        </span>
      </a>
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>

<style>
  /* ---- PADDING ---------------------------------------------------
     FLAT 110px TOP AND BOTTOM AT EVERY WIDTH, per CONTEXT.md's
     per-section table: Cover 64 · About 96 · Educational Leader 110 ·
     Premier 80 · Everyday Legends 70 · Athletic 110 · Feature Quote
     150 · INDEX 110 · Blog 110/40 · Contact 96. Sections MUST NOT
     share a padding token, so the value is set here and nowhere else.

     NO TAPER AND NO svh TERM. The pivot directly above carries a
     clamp — flat 150 in bands 1 and 2, clamp(116px, 14.67vw, 150px)
     below — and About carries a max(96px, 20svh + 12px). Neither
     reason applies here: the pivot's taper exists because 150px of
     padding around one line of type eats a phone viewport, and
     About's svh term exists to keep its lede below an 80svh Cover's
     fold. This section is five rows tall at every width and sits in
     the middle of the scroll. Flat 110, the same value Educational
     Leader and Athletic Management ship.

     NO min-height, NO svh, NO viewport-height floor of any kind
     (R1.4). */
  .index {
    padding-block: 110px;
  }

  /* ---- THE TYPE, AND EVERY AXIS -----------------------------------
     Fraunces defaults to wght 900, WONK ON and SOFT 100 when an axis
     is left unstated. Both tiers below enumerate all four, and
     `font-weight` restates the wght axis in the property the browser
     uses for fallback selection so a Fraunces-less render is not
     900-weight.

     WONK IS 0 ON BOTH. WONK 1 is spent at the Feature Quote and
     nowhere else — it is the pivot's flourish. Numerals set with
     flagged terminals would put the pivot's one signature on the
     page's navigation.

     These are new roles rather than existing --fx-* tiers: the
     numeral is a display-scale FIGURE, which wants opsz 96's sharp
     high-contrast register at wght 300 and SOFT 30, and the row name
     is a small-display role at opsz 24 / wght 400 / SOFT 60 — softer
     and more open than any existing tier, because it is read beside a
     figure roughly twice its size rather than seen alone. Declared
     locally so no other section inherits them.
     ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .index {
    /* R8.2 — DECLARED AGAINST THE LEDE, NOT SWEPT ALONGSIDE IT.
       This was clamp(36px, 3.9vw, 56px) through R8 and R8.1. That
       clamp and the page's --lede-size were two independently swept
       values on different schedules with different floors: --lede-size
       bottoms out at 1368 while the numeral kept falling on 3.9vw down
       to 923. Above 1368 they tracked closely enough to read as
       related; below it they did not, and the measured ratio fell from
       1.023 at 1440 to 0.768 at 1024 and 0.692 at 901 and below —
       outside the 0.90-1.15 window at every width in band 2's lower
       half and all of bands 3 and 4. The relationship was a
       COINCIDENCE OF TWO SCHEDULES that happened to agree in one
       range, not a declaration.

       This project has removed that exact shape three times already —
       --p1-lines, the R4.1 crossover keyed to one element, R6.3's lap
       as a constant. A relationship that must hold is DECLARED. */
    --idx-num-size: calc(var(--lede-size) * 1.02);
    --idx-num-lh: 1;

    --idx-name-size: clamp(20px, 1.95vw, 28px);
    --idx-name-lh: 1.15;

    --idx-desc-size: 11px;
    --idx-desc-lh: 1.6;
    /* The description's line box. Every internal gap in this section
       is a multiple of this or of the name's line box — never a fixed
       pixel value, per R3.1: "Derive internal gaps from the type, not
       from pixels. Fixed pixel gaps set from one width are a swept
       band by another name." */
    --idx-desc-line: calc(var(--idx-desc-size) * var(--idx-desc-lh));

    /* The row's spacing BUDGET. It is spent, never exceeded: the two
       row paddings below always sum to exactly 2x this, so the pitch is
       identical before and after the R8.1 redistribution. */
    --idx-pad: calc(1.15 * var(--idx-desc-line));

    /* ---- INTERNAL LEADING, ONE FORMULA, THREE ROLES (R8.1) --------
       A line box is taller than the cap band it carries, and the
       leftover sits half above the cap and half below the baseline.
       For a face whose cap-height is `c` ems at line-height `lh`, one
       side of that leftover is `(lh - c) * size / 2`.

       c IS 0.700 FOR BOTH FACES ON THIS PAGE. R2 measured Fraunces at
       0.700 from rendered ink with the probe inside its own white
       padding — NOT 0.699 and NOT 0.722, and it is not re-derived here.
       Montserrat's capHeight is 700 per 1000 em, the same figure, so
       one constant serves the numeral, the description and the lede. */
    --idx-num-lead:  calc((var(--idx-num-lh)  - 0.700) * var(--idx-num-size)  / 2);
    --idx-desc-lead: calc((var(--idx-desc-lh) - 0.700) * var(--idx-desc-size) / 2);
    --idx-lede-lead: calc((var(--lede-lh)     - 0.700) * var(--lede-size)     / 2);

    /* THE REDISTRIBUTION. Half the DIFFERENCE between the two leadings
       that bracket a rule — the numeral's above its cap, the
       description's below its baseline — moved from the top padding to
       the bottom one. Half, because the shift closes the gap from both
       ends at once. See the note on .idx-row. */
    --idx-rule-shift: calc((var(--idx-num-lead) - var(--idx-desc-lead)) / 2);
  }

  /* ---- THE LEDE ---------------------------------------------------
     Fraunces at the page's shared --lede-size, clamp(52px, 3.8vw,
     64px) — 54.72px at 1440, the same figure every other lede on the
     page resolves to. EVERY AXIS IS ENUMERATED: opsz 96 · wght 300 ·
     SOFT 30 · WONK 0. Fraunces defaults to wght 900, WONK on and SOFT
     100 when an axis is left unstated, and `font-weight` restates the
     wght axis in the property the browser uses for fallback selection.

     WONK 0. WONK 1 is spent at the Feature Quote and nowhere else.

     Obsidian, and NOT GOLD — the three saturated accents are spent
     (Cover tagline, About drop cap, pull quote) and this is not one of
     them.

     ---- THE GAP TO THE RULE ---------------------------------------
     A rule belongs to what it INTRODUCES, not to what precedes it
     (R3.1, Educational Leader's coda rule: space above ~3.3x space
     below). This rule is the list's top edge, so it bonds downward to
     the numerals and separates upward from the lede — more air above
     it than below it, or it reads as underlining the sentence instead
     of opening the list.

     0.62 of the lede's own line box, so it re-resolves across the whole
     clamp with nothing swept: 40.02px at 1440, 38.06px at 1024, 30.06px
     at the clamp floor. No fixed pixel gap. The clearance BELOW the
     rule is not declared here at all — it is entry 01's row padding-top,
     which is the same quantity sitting below all four row rules. */
  .idx-lede {
    margin: 0 0 calc(0.62 * var(--lede-size) * var(--lede-lh)) 0;
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 96, 'wght' 300, 'SOFT' 30, 'WONK' 0;
    font-weight: 300;
    font-size: var(--lede-size);
    line-height: var(--lede-lh);
    color: var(--obsidian);
  }

  /* THE FIFTH RULE. R8 shipped four — between the five entries, none
     above 01 and none under 05. The lede makes a fifth necessary: the
     list needs a top edge to be a list rather than five names loose
     under a sentence. Still NO rule under entry 05; the section closes
     on air, which is what stops it reading as a boxed table.

     Same 1px Smoked Slate as the row rules, and the same span — the
     <ol> IS the section window, so the rule stops one column short of
     the right trim exactly as the other four do. NO GOLD: the
     hairline-gold bucket stays at 0 of 8 and is re-measured, not
     assumed. */
  .idx-list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--slate);
  }

  /* ---- THE ROW ----------------------------------------------------
     ONE NUMERAL AND ONE TEXT BLOCK ON ONE BASELINE. The row is a
     subgrid of the section (see the primitive block in global.css) and
     carries `align-items: baseline`, so grid's own baseline-sharing
     group answers the alignment.

     THE OFFSET IS NOT MODELLED IN em. R6.3: Chrome rounds a font's
     ascent and descent to whole pixels at the rendered size, so a
     hand-written `lh/2 + (asc - desc)/2` offset drifts unpredictably
     with font-size — measured 0.02px out at 31.68px and 1.27px out at
     26px on this same face. The numeral runs from 36px to 56px across
     the page's widths and the name from 20px to 28px, so an em
     constant would be wrong at most of them. Baseline delta measured
     at 0.00px; see the pass report.

     VERTICAL PADDING IS DERIVED FROM THE DESCRIPTION'S LINE BOX, at
     1.15 of it. It is a calc() over the type tokens above, so moving
     the description's size or leading re-resolves every row's rhythm
     with nothing swept and no breakpoint band.

     ---- THE RULES ARE EQUALIZED ON INK, NOT ON BOXES (R8.1) --------
     R8 set the two paddings equal, which made the two BOX gaps around
     each rule equal and the two INK gaps unequal. A rule has a
     description's line box above it and a numeral's line box below it,
     and neither line box is filled by its glyphs: the description's
     leaves leading under its baseline, the numeral's leaves leading
     above its cap, and the numeral's is the larger because the numeral
     is the larger type. Measured at 1440 with the numeral at wght 400:
     23.63px of ink clearance above each rule against 29.03px below it —
     a 5.40px asymmetry that reads as the rules sitting low. The gap
     grows with the numeral's size, so it is worst at the widest
     viewports and it got worse when the numeral went to wght 400.

     THE CORRECTION IS HALF THE DIFFERENCE OF THE TWO LEADINGS, moved
     from the top padding to the bottom. Half, because moving `d` from
     one side to the other closes the asymmetry by `2d`. Both leadings
     come from the one formula above; nothing here is a swept pixel and
     nothing is measured at one width and frozen.

     THE PITCH DOES NOT CHANGE. The two paddings still sum to
     2 x --idx-pad at every width, so the redistribution spends none of
     the budget it redistributes.

     R6.3 APPLIES AND THE RESULT IS REPORTED, NOT ASSUMED. A modelled em
     offset cannot reliably track a rounded font metric, so the residual
     is measured rather than declared closed; see the pass report for
     the per-rule figures against the 2px window. */
  .idx-row {
    align-items: baseline;
    padding-top:    calc(var(--idx-pad) - var(--idx-rule-shift));
    padding-bottom: calc(var(--idx-pad) + var(--idx-rule-shift));
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
    /* Only opacity transitions anywhere in this section. Never
       transition-all, and never a colour transition — the hover
       states below are instant by specification. */
    transition: opacity 140ms var(--ease);
  }

  /* The whole row is the control on entry 04. The default disclosure
     marker comes off in both engines. */
  summary.idx-row {
    list-style: none;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  summary.idx-row::-webkit-details-marker {
    display: none;
  }

  /* ---- THE NUMERAL ------------------------------------------------
     Fraunces, opsz 96 · wght 300 · SOFT 30 · WONK 0. Obsidian.

     LEFT-ALIGNED INSIDE ITS WINDOW — never centred, never
     right-aligned. The window is three columns of twelve and the
     figures fill roughly a third of it; the air to their right is the
     leader space of a printed contents page and is deliberate. It is
     NOT closed by shrinking the window, which would put the text block
     on a fourth left edge.

     wght 400, RAISED FROM R8's 300 (R8.1). THE COMPOSITION TABLE
     SAYS THE NUMERALS ARE THIS SECTION'S ENTRANCE, and at wght 300
     they were the quietest element in their own row: opsz swaps
     STRUCTURAL DETAIL, not merely size, so the name's low opsz 24
     thickens its strokes while the numeral's opsz 96 goes sharp and
     high-contrast. A 28px name at wght 400 / opsz 24 therefore put
     more ink on the page than a 56px numeral at wght 300 / opsz 96,
     which inverts the hierarchy the table specifies — visible in the
     screenshot before any measurement. At equal weight the numeral is
     twice the name's size and reads as the entrance.

     WEIGHT DOES NOT ENTER THE SIZE RATIO, so R8.1's change to it left
     the numeral's relationship to the lede exactly where R8 put it.

     SCALE IS RELATIONAL, AND SINCE R8.2 IT IS DECLARED RATHER THAN
     MEASURED. --idx-num-size is calc(var(--lede-size) * 1.02), so the
     numeral is 1.02x the page's lede at EVERY width by construction —
     there is no width at which the ratio can drift, because there is
     no second schedule for it to drift against. See the token.

     WHY 1.02 AND NOT 1.00 OR 1.15. The Index is the page's NAVIGATION.
     Numerals set larger than the sections' own opening statements
     would invert the hierarchy, so the ceiling is real; numerals set
     smaller than the lede sitting three inches above them stop being
     the entrance the Composition table says they are, so the floor is
     real too. 1.02 is parity with a hair of authority — the figure R8
     arrived at by measurement at 1440, now held everywhere instead of
     at one width.

     AGAINST THE ROW NAME the numeral is 1.99x at 1440 and RISES as the
     viewport narrows — 2.65x from 1024 down — because the name keeps
     its own clamp while the numeral now follows the lede's. The 1.80
     floor is cleared at every width by a wider margin than before.

     `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums` so 01-05 occupy identical
     advance widths — a contents column whose figures shift left and
     right by the width of a 1 is a contents column with five left
     edges.

     `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums` so 01-05 occupy identical
     advance widths — a contents column whose figures shift left and
     right by the width of a 1 is a contents column with five left
     edges. */
  .idx-num {
    font-family: var(--display);
    /* Literal value unchanged; it now reads from the token declared on
       .idx-list so the resting and hovered ends cannot drift apart. */
    font-variation-settings: var(--idx-num-rest);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: var(--idx-num-size);
    line-height: var(--idx-num-lh);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums lining-nums;
    color: var(--obsidian);
  }

  /* ---- THE TEXT BLOCK ---------------------------------------------
     Its left edge is column 4 in the 12-column bands, which is the
     lede edge — one of CONTEXT.md's three established left edges.
     Nothing in this section lands on a fourth. */
  .idx-body {
    display: block;
  }

  /* The name's line. A flex row so the name and — on entry 04 only —
     the indicator share a baseline and the indicator right-aligns to
     THIS block's right edge rather than to an edge of its own. With
     one child (every row but 04) the name simply sits flush left and
     the flex container's first baseline is the name's, which is what
     the row's baseline group aligns the numeral to. Identical
     structure on all five rows, so all five baselines are computed the
     same way. */
  .idx-line {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--idx-desc-line);
  }

  /* Fraunces, opsz 24 · wght 400 · SOFT 60 · WONK 0. Obsidian. */
  .idx-name {
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 24, 'wght' 400, 'SOFT' 60, 'WONK' 0;
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: var(--idx-name-size);
    line-height: var(--idx-name-lh);
    color: var(--obsidian);
  }

  /* Montserrat 11px / 500 / 0.14em / uppercase, Smoked Slate.

     THE UPPERCASE IS A text-transform. The authored string in the
     markup above is sentence case, exactly as COPY.md writes it.

     DESCRIPTIONS MAY WRAP. They are not the Athletic caption, which
     has to clear a three-column window on one line. A two-line
     description at 390 is correct behaviour, and the type is NOT
     shrunk and the tracking is NOT tightened to prevent it.

     The gap above is 0.30 of the NAME's line box, so it scales with
     the name across the clamp instead of holding one pixel value from
     one width — 9.66px at 1440, 6.90px at 390. */
  .idx-desc {
    display: block;
    margin-top: calc(0.3 * var(--idx-name-size) * var(--idx-name-lh));
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: var(--idx-desc-size);
    line-height: var(--idx-desc-lh);
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--slate);
  }

  /* ---- THE ROW HAIRLINES ------------------------------------------
     FOUR RULES, NOT FIVE AND NOT SIX. A bottom rule on entries 01, 02,
     03 and 04; NO rule under entry 05 and NO rule above entry 01. An
     index that opens and closes with a rule is a boxed table; one that
     only ever appears BETWEEN entries is a contents page.

     THE RULE IS ON THE <li>, NOT ON THE ROW. On entry 04 the <details>
     is inside the <li>, so the rule sits below the expansion and moves
     down when it opens. The rule closes the ENTRY, not the summary.

     1px Smoked Slate. NO GOLD — see the header comment. */
  .idx-item:not(:last-child) {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--slate);
  }

  /* ---- INTERACTIVE STATES -----------------------------------------
     Every clickable element gets hover, focus-visible and active.

     HOVER: the name takes a 1px underline and the row's hairline goes
     Obsidian. BOTH INSTANT — no colour transition anywhere. The
     hairline lives on the <li>, so the row's hover has to reach it
     through :has(); that is why the selector is shaped this way rather
     than sitting on .idx-row:hover.
     ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .idx-row:hover .idx-name {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
    text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
  }

  /* FOCUS JOINS HOVER ON THE ROW-LEVEL STATES (R13.1).
     R13 established that keyboard focus produces the same weight shift
     as hover. R13.1 adds two more row-level responses, so focus has to
     take them too — otherwise a keyboard user gets the numeral and the
     rule thickening while the rule's COLOUR stays slate, which paints a
     0.5px obsidian line on top of a 1px slate one. The colour rule's
     trigger is extended rather than a second rule added, so the two
     states cannot drift.

     THE THICKENING IS INSTANT AND CARRIES NO TRANSITION. Every hover
     state in this section is documented as instant, and a transition on
     box-shadow would be a second animated property outside the scoped
     exception, which covers font-variation-settings only. */
  .idx-item:has(.idx-row:hover),
  .idx-item:has(.idx-row:focus-visible) {
    border-bottom-color: var(--obsidian);
    box-shadow: inset 0 calc(-1 * var(--idx-rule-add)) 0 0 var(--obsidian);
  }

  /* The numeral. Same trigger, same instant response, opposite
     direction to the name. */
  .idx-row:hover .idx-num,
  .idx-row:focus-visible .idx-num {
    font-variation-settings: var(--idx-num-hover);
  }

  .idx-row:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--slate);
    outline-offset: 4px;
  }

  .idx-row:active {
    opacity: 0.7;
  }

  /* ---- R13 — THE WEIGHT SHIFT -------------------------------------
     THE ONE SCOPED EXCEPTION IN THE BUILD. CONTEXT.md's Standing Rules
     permit `font-variation-settings` to be transitioned on INDEX ENTRY
     NAMES ONLY and nowhere else on the page, conditional on a measured
     pass. It is a layout-affecting property; every other transition in
     this file is opacity.

     RESTING AND HOVERED VALUES ARE THE SYSTEM'S, NOT THE REFERENCE'S.
     The 21st.dev component this mechanism is adapted from animates
     'wght' 400 -> 700. 700 does not exist in this build's optical
     system: the Fraunces table's body-adjacent/small row (opsz 9-24,
     SOFT 60-80) states no weight at all, and the shipped tier for that
     role, --fx-small, sits at wght 420. 420 is the heaviest weight
     anywhere in the seven --fx-* tiers, whose whole range is 300-420.
     So the move is 400 -> 420 and it is deliberately small. A larger
     one is off-system and needs a ruling that widens the range, not a
     constant chosen inside a build pass.

     EVERY AXIS IS ENUMERATED AT BOTH ENDS. Per the permanent Fraunces
     gotcha: an unstated axis is not inherited across a transition, it
     reverts to the face's own default, and Fraunces defaults to SOFT
     100 and WONK on. A transition naming only `wght` would silently
     ship maximum roundness and flagged terminals at the hovered end.
     opsz, SOFT and WONK are therefore restated at their RESTING values
     on the hovered side and do not move: the brief animates weight,
     and a second travelling axis changes letterform shape rather than
     weight.

     :global() IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT DEFENSIVE. `.idx-ch` is created by
     the script at the foot of this file, so it never receives Astro's
     data-astro-cid-* attribute and a scoped selector would silently
     stop matching. This is the exact failure CONTEXT.md records twice
     already (<Image> in E.5, the Phase F status line).

     THE STAGGER IS AUTHORED AS DATA, NOT AS CSS. Each character span
     carries --d, its integer distance from the centre of the string,
     written once at split time. The delay is that distance times the
     step, so the centre character leads and the two ends arrive last,
     symmetrically. Nothing here re-measures on resize.
     ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .idx-list {
    /* R13.1 — THE CEILING IS RULED UP, FOR THIS INTERACTION ONLY.
       R13 shipped 400 -> 420 because 420 is --fx-small, the heaviest
       weight in the shipped optical system, and the system's whole
       range is 300-420. At reading size that move was invisible.
       Jackson has ruled the ceiling up to 700 for INDEX ENTRY NAMES
       ONLY. It does not raise the ceiling anywhere else on the page and
       it does not alter --fx-small, which still governs every other
       body-adjacent role. */
    --idx-wght-rest:  'opsz' 24, 'wght' 400, 'SOFT' 60, 'WONK' 0;
    --idx-wght-hover: 'opsz' 24, 'wght' 700, 'SOFT' 60, 'WONK' 0;

    /* COUNTERWEIGHT 1 — THE NUMERAL MOVES THE OTHER WAY.
       The numeral is a display-scale FIGURE at opsz 96, and the
       optical system table puts a display role's weight band at
       300-400. Its resting 400 is that band's CEILING, so the only
       in-system travel available to it is downward. 300 is the band's
       floor and --fx-hero's own weight.

       That the only in-system direction is also the right one is not a
       coincidence: on a printed contents page the entry you are about
       to turn to gains weight and the marker beside it recedes. The
       row transfers emphasis rather than simply getting louder, which
       is what stops one word looking fat.

       opsz, SOFT and WONK do not move here either. */
    --idx-num-rest:  'opsz' 96, 'wght' 400, 'SOFT' 30, 'WONK' 0;
    --idx-num-hover: 'opsz' 96, 'wght' 300, 'SOFT' 30, 'WONK' 0;

    /* COUNTERWEIGHT 2 — THE RULE THICKENS BY HALF ITS OWN WEIGHT.
       DERIVED, NOT PICKED. The name's stem width scales roughly with
       wght, so a rule tracking the name proportionally would go
       1 -> 1.75px and stop being a hairline. Half a pixel is the
       smallest increment a 2x display can actually resolve (3 device
       pixels against 2) and half the rule's own weight — a fraction of
       the rule, which is what the brief asks for, rather than a
       fraction of the name's travel.

       IT IS AN INSET box-shadow, NOT A BORDER-WIDTH. The rule is
       `border-bottom` on .idx-item, so thickening the border would add
       0.5px to the item's height and fail the row-height criterion
       outright. An inset shadow paints inside the padding box, sits
       directly on top of the border, and contributes nothing to
       layout. Verified at 0.00px in the pass report. */
    --idx-rule-add: 0.5px;

    --idx-ch-dur: 260ms;
    --idx-ch-step: 30ms;
  }

  /* `display: inline` and nothing else. An inline-block would create a
     line box per character and hand the browser a break opportunity at
     every glyph, which is how this mechanism usually ships a name that
     wraps mid-word. Inline spans add no break opportunities: breaking
     is computed from the text content, which is unchanged. */
  .idx-name :global(.idx-ch) {
    display: inline;
    font-variation-settings: var(--idx-wght-rest);
    transition: font-variation-settings var(--idx-ch-dur) var(--ease);
    transition-delay: calc(var(--d, 0) * var(--idx-ch-step));
  }

  /* FOCUS PRODUCES THE SAME SHIFT AS HOVER — the same declaration, not
     a parallel one that can drift. Entry 04's row is a <summary> and is
     focusable in its own right, so all five rows are reachable. */
  .idx-row:hover .idx-name :global(.idx-ch),
  .idx-row:focus-visible .idx-name :global(.idx-ch) {
    font-variation-settings: var(--idx-wght-hover);
  }

  /* REDUCED MOTION: no stagger, no transition. The weight still resolves
     on hover — the preference asks for no animation, not for no state.
     Both the duration and the step go to zero, so the shift is instant
     and every character arrives together. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .idx-list {
      --idx-ch-dur: 0s;
      --idx-ch-step: 0s;
    }
    .idx-name :global(.idx-ch) {
      transition: none;
    }
  }

  /* ---- THE INDICATOR ----------------------------------------------
     Montserrat, AT THE NAME'S SIZE AND WEIGHT (R8.1), Smoked Slate,
     right-aligned to the text block's right edge, becoming − (U+2212,
     the true minus, not a hyphen) under [open].

     R8 SET IT AT DESCRIPTION SCALE AND THAT WAS TOO SMALL TO FUNCTION.
     An 11px glyph rendered 6.41px wide at 1440 and sat 866px from the
     name it belongs to: at that size and that distance it reads as a
     speck on the page rather than as the control for the one row whose
     behaviour differs. The right-alignment itself is correct and is
     kept — it is the section's one sanctioned right alignment, and its
     job is to mark the row that is not a link.

     THE FIX IS SIZE, NOT POSITION. At the name's own clamp and weight
     the glyph is a peer of the thing it modifies rather than a mark
     beside it, and it needs no colour change, no box, no rule and no
     motion to be found. It stays Smoked Slate — one value quieter than
     the Obsidian name — so it announces itself without competing.

     IT SHARES THE NAME'S BASELINE THROUGH THE EXISTING GROUP, not
     through an offset. `.idx-line` is `align-items: baseline`, so the
     glyph and the name are in one flex baseline group and the layout
     engine answers the alignment. Per R6.3 an em constant cannot track
     a rounded font metric, and both now run the same clamp from 20px
     to 28px, so a modelled offset would be wrong at most widths.

     NO ROTATION, NO TRANSITION, NO TRANSFORM. A rotating + is a
     component convention; this page is a printed spread and a printed
     spread's glyphs do not spin. The swap is a content change on a
     pseudo-element, which is why it needs no script. NOT GOLD.

     `flex-shrink: 0` so a long name never squeezes the glyph, and the
     span is aria-hidden — <details> already exposes aria-expanded and
     a screen reader announcing "plus" adds nothing. */
  .idx-sign {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: var(--idx-name-size);
    line-height: var(--idx-name-lh);
    color: var(--slate);
  }
  .idx-sign::before {
    content: '+';
  }
  .idx-details[open] .idx-sign::before {
    content: '\2212';
  }

  /* ---- THE EXPANSION ----------------------------------------------
     Data only, no prose. It sits on .idx-body, so it starts on the
     same left edge as the names — no sub-columns, no new left edges.

     THE GAP ABOVE THE FIRST BROTHER IS NOT THE GAP BETWEEN BROTHERS,
     AND THE FIRST ROUND SHIPPED IT AS ONE. The summary already carries
     its own 1.15-line bottom padding, and the brothers are 1.15 lines
     apart from each other, so with no padding here the expansion's
     first row sat exactly as far below "JAYLEN, KJ & CAM" as "KJ
     McClain" sits below Jaylen's program line — measured 37px against
     37px at 1440. Four items at one rhythm read as one list, and the
     description belongs to the SUMMARY, not to the brothers.

     0.62 lines on top of the summary's 1.15 makes the boundary 1.77
     lines against the list's own 1.15 — a 1.54:1 step, which is the
     smallest ratio that reads as a level change rather than as a
     slightly loose gap. Derived from the description's line box like
     every other gap in this section; nothing swept, no line count.

     The bottom gap TRACKS THE ROW'S BOTTOM PADDING rather than the raw
     budget (R8.1), so entry 04's rule closes the expansion on exactly
     the ink clearance it closes a shut row on. Its "above the rule"
     element is a brother's program line, which is the description's
     size and leading, so the same equalization applies unchanged. */
  .idx-expand {
    padding-top: calc(0.62 * var(--idx-desc-line));
    padding-bottom: calc(var(--idx-pad) + var(--idx-rule-shift));
  }

  .idx-bros {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .idx-bro + .idx-bro {
    margin-top: calc(1.15 * var(--idx-desc-line));
  }

  /* Line 1: name left, domain right-aligned on the SAME LINE, sharing
     a baseline through flex's baseline alignment. The domain aligns to
     the text block's right edge — the one right-hand alignment this
     section permits, and it is an alignment to an edge that already
     exists rather than a new one.

     In bands 3 and 4 this becomes a block and the domain drops beneath
     the name; see the collapse block at the foot of this file. */
  .idx-bro-line {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--idx-desc-line);
  }

  .idx-bro-name {
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: 13px;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--obsidian);
  }

  /* ONE RULE FOR ALL THREE DOMAINS, LINKED OR NOT (R8.1). This is the
     whole visual treatment and it is applied by `.idx-bro-site`, which
     both the two <a>s and Cam's <span> carry. Face, size, tracking,
     case, colour and right-alignment are therefore identical by
     construction rather than by two rules kept in agreement by hand —
     the failure mode CONTEXT.md flags when two elements are declared
     separately and expected to render the same. */
  .idx-bro-site {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 11px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: lowercase;
    color: var(--slate);
  }

  /* THE LINKED ONES, AND ONLY THE LINKED ONES. `.idx-bro-link` carries
     no visual property at rest beyond removing the UA underline — it
     exists solely to hold the interactive states, so a domain that goes
     from plain text to a link changes nothing a reader sees until they
     point at it.

     States match the index rows exactly: hover a 1px underline, instant;
     focus-visible a 2px Smoked Slate outline at 4px offset; active
     opacity 0.7. transition on OPACITY ONLY — never transition-all and
     never a colour transition. */
  .idx-bro-link {
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: opacity 140ms var(--ease);
  }
  .idx-bro-link:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
    text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
  }
  .idx-bro-link:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--slate);
    outline-offset: 4px;
  }
  .idx-bro-link:active {
    opacity: 0.7;
  }

  /* Line 2: the program. School names as plain text — the OSU interim
     rule is in force for all three schools: no logos, no marks, no
     team photography, no school-keyed colour. */
  .idx-bro-prog {
    display: block;
    margin-top: calc(0.3 * var(--idx-desc-line));
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: 11px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--slate);
  }

  /* ---- BANDS 3 AND 4 ----------------------------------------------
     THE DOMAIN DROPS BENEATH THE NAME rather than right-aligning. At
     eight tracks the text block is five of eight — 556.5px at 1023 and
     421.4px at 751 — and "University of Tennessee Football | #40" plus
     "kjmcclain.com" on one line leaves a leader gap of a few
     characters, which reads as a failed justification rather than as
     an alignment. Below 750 there is one column and the question does
     not arise.

     Keyed at 1023 so it covers bands 3 and 4 together; there is no
     separate 750 rule, because a block is a block in both. */
  @media (max-width: 1023px) {
    .idx-bro-line {
      display: block;
    }
    .idx-bro-site {
      display: block;
      margin-top: calc(0.2 * var(--idx-desc-line));
    }
  }

  /* Band 4 only. The numeral takes its own line above the text block
     — that is the grid primitive's doing, in global.css — and the two
     need a gap between them that the row's baseline group no longer
     provides once they are on separate rows. Derived from the
     numeral's own line box, not from a pixel. */
  @media (max-width: 750px) {
    .idx-row {
      row-gap: calc(0.34 * var(--idx-num-size) * var(--idx-num-lh));
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  /* ---- R13 — CHARACTER SPLIT FOR THE WEIGHT SHIFT ------------------
     The mechanism only, adapted from the 21st.dev React component named
     in the brief: per-character wrapping with a transition delay
     proportional to distance from the centre of the string. Written
     natively. NO framer-motion, NO GSAP, no animation dependency — the
     transition is CSS and this script's whole job is to write --d.

     NO SCROLL LISTENER. The build has exactly one, R12's, and this adds
     nothing to it. The only listener here is on a MediaQueryList.

     ---- WHY THE STRING IS DUPLICATED --------------------------------
     Splitting a string into per-character elements is the standard way
     this effect breaks a screen reader: some engines stop treating the
     run as one string and spell the name out letter by letter. So the
     name ships twice —

       .vh          the intact string, in the accessibility tree, clipped
                    out of the visual render and out of flow
       .idx-glyphs  the split characters, aria-hidden, carrying the ink

     The link's accessible name is computed from its contents, so it
     still resolves to the same string it did before this pass: the
     numeral is already aria-hidden, .idx-glyphs is now aria-hidden, and
     .vh supplies the name. Verified against the rendered accessibility
     tree at every width, not asserted.

     .vh is position:absolute with clip-path:inset(50%), so it
     contributes no layout: it cannot move the left edge, the right edge
     or the row height. That is why the intact copy is safe to add.

     ---- WHY IT IS GATED ON (hover: hover) AND (pointer: fine) --------
     Touch gets no hover state AND no wrapping side effects, so on a
     touch device the script does not run at all and the markup is left
     exactly as authored. Gating only the CSS would still ship the split
     DOM to a phone, which is a side effect. The listener re-runs the
     check if the capability changes under a hybrid device rather than
     leaving it decided at first paint.

     ---- SPACES ARE NOT WRAPPED --------------------------------------
     A space stays a bare text node between two spans. Wrapping it would
     put a non-breaking decision in the middle of the name's only legal
     break point; leaving it bare keeps the break opportunities
     byte-identical to the unsplit string, which is what holds the row
     to one line and therefore holds the row height.

     --d COUNTS OVER THE WHOLE STRING, SPACES INCLUDED, so the stagger
     is geometric about the centre of the name rather than about the
     centre of its letters.
     ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
  const FINE = window.matchMedia('(hover: hover) and (pointer: fine)');

  function split() {
    const names = document.querySelectorAll('#sec-index .idx-name');
    names.forEach((el) => {
      if (el.querySelector('.idx-glyphs')) return; // already split
      const text = el.textContent ?? '';
      const chars = Array.from(text);
      const centre = (chars.length - 1) / 2;

      const intact = document.createElement('span');
      intact.className = 'vh';
      intact.textContent = text;

      const glyphs = document.createElement('span');
      glyphs.className = 'idx-glyphs';
      glyphs.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');

      chars.forEach((c, i) => {
        if (c === ' ') {
          glyphs.appendChild(document.createTextNode(' '));
          return;
        }
        const s = document.createElement('span');
        s.className = 'idx-ch';
        s.style.setProperty('--d', String(Math.abs(i - centre)));
        s.textContent = c;
        glyphs.appendChild(s);
      });

      el.textContent = '';
      el.append(intact, glyphs);
    });
  }

  if (FINE.matches) split();
  FINE.addEventListener('change', (e) => {
    if (e.matches) split();
  });
</script>
